1.	Interfaces have a contract driven design meaning they
	are declarations, and don't give any hint on implementation.
	They encapsulate what and object can do, but not how it does
	it. They also foster polymorphism.	
		Abstract classes offer a more rigid set of instructions
	on how an object can be used. Also only allow one abstract
	class to be implemented.
	
2.	Interfaces are called a programming contract as they define 
	what methods a class has to implement. This is done to enforce
	consistency, allowing for flexibility. The breaking of this
	contract can result in code failing, unexpected errors, or
	unexpected data being used. Can also result in incorrect outputs.
	
3.	Interfaces are used when consistency is needed across unrelated 
	classes. Class inheritance is used when a group of related classes
	need some common behaviours, but still need private, unrelated
	implementation.
	
4.	Lambda expressions are a block of code that takes in parameters
	and returns a value. They make interfaces more convenient to
	use by making them more readable and less verbose.